WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Norman Borlog's dwarf wheat ended India's grain crises, but left Punjab's water table dropping nearly a meter a year. This is unintended consequences, episode 101. Welcome to the very first episode. This show tells the stories of inventions, policies and systems that began with clear good intentions, yet produced results no one quite expected. The world already has plenty of tales that treat past decisions as simple mistakes. This series takes a different approach.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:50.380
It looks at the information available at the time, the pressure's decision makers faced, and the chain of events that followed. Each traces the same six steps, the original problem, the chosen response, the rollout, the surprises that appeared later, the efforts to adjust, and the principle worth carrying forward.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:01:14.940
Today's case begins in the mid-1960s, when India confronted repeated grain shortages and turned to a new set of wheat varieties developed in Mexico. In the villages of Punjab's Malwa region, farmers once lowered their pumps a few meters each season to reach water. Today, many must drill more than 200 meters, and the electricity that runs those pumps still arrives free of charge.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:25.160
The same high-yielding wheat and rice varieties that lifted the country out of recurrent famine now require an extraction race that the underlying aquifer cannot sustain.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:35.480
Norman Borlog developed short-stemmed, fertilizer-responsive wheat lines in Mexico during the 1950s and 1960s. His goal was straightforward.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:50.920
Raised cereal yields fast enough to outrun population growth and prevent the large-scale famines that had already struck parts of Asia. When India faced severe grain shortages in the mid-1960s, the government invited Borlogs methods and seeds.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:59.020
Policy makers saw the new varieties as a practical way to achieve food self-sufficiency without depending indefinitely on imports.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:13.240
At the time, the limiting factor appeared to be biological yield, not long-term water or soil constraints. The approach therefore emphasized irrigation and nutrient supply as the necessary compliments to the new genetics.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:24.400
Decision makers reasoned that if the plant could convert more fertilizer into grain without lodging, then the immediate task was to ensure those inputs reached the field in reliable quantities.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:33.940
They treated water and nutrients as adjustable variables that could be scaled through infrastructure and price policy, rather than as fixed environmental limits.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:43.440
This framing made sense because the visible crisis was empty granaries and import bills, not yet declining well levels or rising soil salinity.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.820
The logic chain ran from genetics to agronomy to public finance.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:54.400
Better plants plus more water plus more fertilizer would close the gap between domestic production and consumption.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:03.680
India imported Borlogs wheat lines in 1966 and rapidly multiplied seed for the 1967 to 1968 season.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:11.020
Punjab, with its relatively reliable canal network and energetic farming community, became the flagship state.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:20.180
State and central governments introduced subsidies on urea fertilizer and crucially, free or near free electricity for agricultural tube wells.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:30.640
Early harvest exceeded expectations. India moved from chronic deficits to self-sufficiency in wheat by the mid-1970s and later to modest exports.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:40.240
Agricultural scientists and officials described the period as the green revolution and credited the combination of seeds, water, and fertilizer for the turnaround.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:49.760
The rollout proceeded through state seed corporations, extension services, and procurement guarantees that assured farmers a market for the expanded output.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:58.020
Because the new varieties performed best under controlled moisture, the expansion of tube wells accelerated in tandem with seed distribution.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:06.040
Procurement prices for wheat and later rice were set to reward volume, reinforcing the choice to plant those two crops in sequence.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:18.360
Early yield gains were measured in quintals per hectare and reported as proof that the input package worked, the accounting focused on annual production totals rather than cumulative aquifer drawdown.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:28.360
Skeptics at the time raised questions about long-term soil health, yet the immediate success in closing the food gap overshadowed those cautions in policy circles.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:38.480
The new varieties demanded consistent moisture during critical growth stages, so farmers expanded winter wheat and later, summer rice on the same land.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:45.380
Cheap power removed any direct cost for pumping, while subsidized urea kept nutrient expenses low.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:54.440
Over successive decades the rice wheat rotation became locked in because markets, procurement prices, and input subsidies all reinforced it.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.440
Groundwater levels in central Punjab began a steady decline.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.880
State monitoring showed average drops approaching 1 meter per year in many blocks.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:13.400
As the water table fell, salts that had been flushed downward by irrigation water rose again through capillary action, salinizing surface soils.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:21.340
Farmers responded by drilling deeper and running pumps longer, increasing energy demand even while electricity remained free.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:29.620
The rotation itself continued because switching to less water intensive crops would reduce income under the prevailing price and procurement system.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:38.920
Second order effects now include declining well yields, higher capital costs for deeper pumps, and reduced flexibility for future crop choices.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.000
The arithmetic of aquifer works like this.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:50.600
Each additional meter of drawdown requires proportionally more energy to lift the same volume, yet the marginal cost to the farmer stays zero.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:06:01.240
Because rice needs standing water during the hottest months, summer pumping volumes grew faster than winter pumping, concentrating extraction in the season when recharge is lowest.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:09.740
Salinization follows because excess irrigation without adequate drainage leaves behind dissolved minerals once the water evaporates.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:16.160
The deeper the water table, the harder it becomes for natural rainfall to flush those salts below the root zone.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:26.120
Over time, the system rewards farmers who can afford deeper wells and punishes those who cannot, concentrating land and water access among larger operators.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:37.560
The original yield gains therefore rested on a subsidy structure that treated the aquifer as an open access resource whose depletion carried no price signal back to the individual decision maker.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:48.020
Punjobs agricultural universities and state agencies have promoted crop diversification toward maize, pulses, and cotton, yet adoption remains limited.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:57.700
The central government has experimented with direct cash transfers that would replace power subsidies, but political resistance from farm groups has slowed implementation.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:07.060
Some districts now restrict new tube well connections and encourage micro irrigation, yet the rice wheat area has not contracted significantly.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:14.600
Estimates suggest that without changes in incentives, parts of the aquifer could reach critically low levels within 20 years.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.860
Current efforts therefore focus on adjusting the same subsidy structures that once enabled the yield gains.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:35.900
Pilot programs have tested whether paying farmers to leave landfallow or to adopt shorter duration varieties can reduce summer pumping, but these measures operate at the margins of an economic framework still built around wheat and rice procurement.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:48.200
Attempts to meter electricity or introduced tiered tariffs have repeatedly encountered organized opposition because any increase in power cost directly reduces net returns under the existing crop prices.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:08:01.860
The policy conversation has shifted from how to grow more to how to grow differently without lowering incomes, yet the institutional machinery of minimum support prices and input subsidies remains tuned to the original rotation.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:09.700
Incentive structures that treat one scarce resource as free, while another is abundant will eventually exhaust the free resource.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:17.020
Complex biological and hydrological systems respond to price signals more reliably than to exhortation alone.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:25.800
When a successful intervention creates new dependencies, redesigning the supporting policies requires as much attention as the original innovation.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:36.560
The question today is whether similar yield enhancing technologies in other regions will be paired with realistic pricing for water and power before extraction limits are reached.

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:46.340
We'll leave it there for today, thanks for thinking through this one with me. If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way and I'll see you tomorrow with another.

